NAME
       ssh-add - give private keys to the ssh agent

SYNOPSIS
       ssh-add [-l] [-L] [-d FILE] [-D] [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       ssh-add loads private keys into a running ssh-agent, so that you type
       each key's passphrase once and later ssh logins use the key without
       asking. Without a FILE it adds the default keys (~/.ssh/id_ed25519,
       ~/.ssh/id_rsa and the others that exist). ssh-add finds the agent
       through the SSH_AUTH_SOCK environment variable; without an agent it
       says it could not open a connection to your authentication agent.

OPTIONS
       -l     List the fingerprints of the keys the agent holds.

       -L     List the public keys the agent holds.

       -d FILE
              Remove this key from the agent.

       -D     Remove all keys from the agent.

EXAMPLES
       Start an agent in this shell and add your key:

           eval $(ssh-agent)
           ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519

NOTES
       The RHCSA tasks usually ask for keys without a passphrase, which need
       no agent at all.

SEE ALSO
       ssh-agent(1), ssh-keygen(1), ssh(1)
